## Ported from babashka/ffi, and altered

`src/raylib/scenes/helitorus.cljc`, `src/raylib/scenes/doom.cljc` (with
`doom/map.cljc`, `doom/ray.cljc` and `doom/hud.cljc`) and
`src/raylib/scenes/pacman.cljc` are each a port of raylib-jlt's port of a
program from the babashka/ffi examples directory, not of one of raylib's own
examples. Each is an altered version of both.

    https://github.com/babashka/ffi
    Copyright (c) 2026 Michiel Borkent (@borkdude)
    MIT License

    helitorus  <- examples/helitorus.clj  (1034e56, 2026-08-28)
    doom       <- examples/doom.clj       (1034e56, 2026-08-28)
    pacman     <- examples/pacman.clj     (94ccd77, 2026-08-31)

For doom, the sixteen level rows, the DDA, the shading and the HUD came from
its original; this port draws flat strips instead of textures, casts 180
columns and replaces keys and mouse with touch.

The geometry, the painter's ordering, the backface test, the lighting and the
palette came from the helitorus original and are substantially unchanged. What the
ports changed is how the program reaches raylib and its input: babashka.ffi
calls became this project's wrapper, the buffers gained jolt type hints, the
loop is inverted so the host owns it, and the mouse and keys became touch. The
palette, hsl(337..349, 100..78%, 17..72%), is the quickest way to check the
claim, since it is the original's.

Michiel Borkent's own header for helitorus credits it one step further back, to
a Scittle demo drawing the same figure to a 2D canvas. That attribution is
repeated here rather than independently verified.

